# Cookframe environment — placeholders only. Copy to .env and fill locally.
# Real secrets never live in the repository (see SECURITY.md).

# The active model provider for the capture/normalization pipeline. Selects
# which <PROVIDER>_API_KEY below is read, so a provider swap is one line here
# and never a rename of the secret. One of: openai (add others as adopted).
MODEL_PROVIDER=openai

# Provider API key(s) — only the active provider's key is required. Named per
# provider (the convention the provider SDKs expect), so several can coexist.
OPENAI_API_KEY=replace-me

# The port the instance binds (CFV1-RUN). There is no default: an instance that
# binds a port nobody chose is one its operator cannot reach and cannot tell
# apart from one that did not start.
PORT=8080

# The model the capture and normalization stages are charged against.
OPENAI_MODEL=replace-me

# The secret the iOS Shortcut presents to submit a capture (PDR-0003). At least
# 32 characters; the instance refuses to start with a shorter one.
COOKFRAME_INGEST_CREDENTIAL=replace-me

# The secret that opens the library and recipe pages (ADR-0024). It must be a
# DIFFERENT value from the one above, and the instance refuses to start if the
# two match: PDR-0003 scopes the phone's credential to submission, so losing the
# device must not cost the library.
COOKFRAME_LIBRARY_CREDENTIAL=replace-me

# The directory captured photographs are kept in (ADR-0009). Required, with no
# default: PDR-0001 keeps a photograph until the capture-quality gate passes, and
# an instance with nowhere to keep one refuses to start rather than lose it. It
# is written to once at start, before the port opens, so a directory that cannot
# be written stops the instance saying so. Back it up with the database.
STORAGE_ROOT=./.data

# The address this instance answers at FROM OUTSIDE, which every capability URL
# is built on (ADR-0016). Bring fetches that URL server-side from its own
# infrastructure (ADR-0017), so this has to be the public name — behind a reverse
# proxy, the proxy's address, including any path prefix it serves under. It is
# REQUIRED and has no default: an absent value, a relative one, a scheme other
# than http/https, a password in it, or a query or fragment on it are each
# refused by name at start. `http` is accepted, and its cost is yours to weigh:
# the capability token sits in the URL's path and travels in clear over it.
PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://example.invalid

# The PostgreSQL database the instance keeps its library in (ADR-0015). There is
# no default: an absent value is refused at the seam rather than falling back to
# a local database nobody chose, because a fallback lets a misconfigured
# instance come up and appear to work.
#
# Apply the schema once, by hand, before first start — EVERY file in
# migrations/, in filename order, not just the first:
#   for m in migrations/*.sql; do psql "$DATABASE_URL" -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 -f "$m"; done
# Each file also carries its own psql line at the top.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://cookframe:replace-me@localhost:5432/cookframe

# DATABASE_URL is REQUIRED, and it is the one required value `src/server/config.ts`
# does not list: the store's own seam reads and refuses it (`resolveDatabaseUrl`),
# which also rejects a URL no PostgreSQL driver could connect with. An instance
# started without it exits non-zero naming the variable, and one started against a
# database the migration was never applied to exits naming the migration file —
# before it binds a port, so a misconfigured instance never looks healthy
# (ADR-0027).
